    "System.CreatedDate": "2026-09-03T18:15:53.61Z",
    "System.Description": "<div><b>As a</b> Report Author </div><div><b>I want</b> to link Date Range and Formula Range content into my report template </div><div><b>So that</b> the report can display computed/structural content (like a date series or formula-derived summary) rather than a single variable's value </div><div><br> </div><div><b>Size estimate:</b> TBD � shell created for estimation; Formula Range portion blocked pending investigation </div><div><b>Sequencing note:</b> None for Date Range. Formula Range is blocked � see open question below. </div><div><br> </div><div><b>Requirements</b> </div><div><br> </div><div><b>Conceptual distinction from variable linking</b> </div><div>Ranges (Date Range, Formula Range) add content to the report rather than mapping a single variable's value into a cell � this is distinct from the variable linking mechanism covered in 1869322. </div><div><br> </div><div><b>Date Range link</b> </div><div>Author can create a Date Range link (WPF: Ranges &gt; Date Range &gt; Link), producing a range of row/column labels tied to the report run period (e.g. Total/2020/2021 rows generated from a linked range). Variable Type for a Date Range link is &quot;Periodic Date&quot;. Confirmed working example: Range =Sheet1!$C$24:$C$26 produces Total/2020/2021 labels referenced by Periodic Links elsewhere in the template. </div><div><br> </div><div><b>Formula Range link � open question, functionality not yet understood</b> </div><div>Web Report Designer must also support Formula Range links (WPF: Ranges &gt; Formula Range &gt; Link), producing &lt;SUMMARY&gt;, &lt;FIRST VALUE&gt;, and &lt;SECOND VALUE&gt; placeholders. It is unclear whether Formula Range ties to a spreadsheet formula's output, an auto-derived calculation over a range (e.g. min/max or start/end of a series), or something else. Neither Marg nor Claude could determine this from UAT inspection. Needs investigation with engineering before AC can be written for this portion. </div>",
